{"title":"Converting Existing LaTeX Notes","markdown":{"headingText":"Converting Existing LaTeX Notes","containsRefs":false,"markdown":"\nFortunately, a large proportion of LaTeX mathematics can be copied directly into Quarto documents without modification.\n\nFor example:\n\n```latex\n\\[\nF = ma\n\\]\n```\n\nand\n\n```latex\n\\begin{equation}\nE = mc^2\n\\end{equation}\n```\n\ntypically work immediately.\n\n## Recommended Migration Approach\n\nRather than converting an entire module at once:\n\n1. Choose a single lecture.\n2. Copy content into a `.qmd` file.\n3. Render frequently.\n4. Fix issues incrementally.\n\nCommon areas that may need attention include:\n\n- TikZ diagrams\n- Custom macros\n- Complex tables\n- Package-specific formatting\n\n## Automatically converting LaTeX\n\nPandoc can convert between lots of different formats including LaTeX (.tex) and Quarto (.qmd). **It won't do a perfect job** and which is why it's recommended to carefully go through your TeX source. However if your TeX is *relatively clean* (few macros, extra packages, complex float layouts), then Pandoc can get you going quite quickly.\n\nFrom the command line you can run\n\n```bash\npandoc -f latex -t markdown input-tex.tex -o output-md.qmd\n```\n\n\n::: {.callout-important}\n\nYou will need to review the output .qmd to make sure you have output that is compatible. \n\n:::\n\n\n::: {.callout-caution}\n\nPandoc is not aware of your whole TeX project: you need to convert each .TeX separarely.\n\n:::\n\n## Tidying up the .qmd file\nHere are some things Pandoc does not readily convert into markdown:\n\n- Section etc labels - if you used underscores in any of your latex labels you should change these to ‘-’ in the markdown file. The underscores confuse markdown. @sec-sections discusses sections, chapters, etc.\n- Figures - the default pandoc conversion is quite limited, so here we’ll use a more adaptable figure environment. In practice its easiest to just copy-paste the example code in @sec-figures.\n- Equations - most equations convert fine but if you want to have equation numbers and labels you’ll need to do some tidying. See @sec-maths for the syntax.\n- Tables - tables have a different format but it’s pretty easy to convert a latex table using find/replace or online table editors/converters (e.g. [Table Convert](https://tableconvert.com/latex-to-markdown))\nReferences - generally easy to fix - see @sec-referencing\n\nThis list is based on the [Making Lecture Notes with Bookdown guide by Vicky Scowcroft](https://vickyscowcroft.github.io/bookdown_lecture_notes_guide/02-latex-conversion.html#tidying)\n\n","srcMarkdownNoYaml":""},"formats":{"html":{"identifier":{"display-name":"HTML","target-format":"html","base-format":"html"},"execute":{"fig-width":7,"fig-height":5,"fig-format":"retina","fig-dpi":96,"df-print":"default","error":false,"eval":true,"cache":null,"freeze":false,"echo":true,"output":true,"warning":true,"include":true,"keep-md":false,"keep-ipynb":false,"ipynb":null,"enabled":null,"daemon":null,"daemon-restart":false,"debug":false,"ipynb-filters":[],"ipynb-shell-interactivity":null,"plotly-connected":true,"engine":"markdown"},"render":{"keep-tex":false,"keep-typ":false,"keep-source":false,"keep-hidden":false,"prefer-html":false,"output-divs":true,"output-ext":"html","fig-align":"default","fig-pos":null,"fig-env":null,"code-fold":false,"code-overflow":"wrap","code-link":false,"code-line-numbers":false,"code-tools":false,"tbl-colwidths":"auto","merge-includes":true,"inline-includes":false,"preserve-yaml":false,"latex-auto-mk":true,"latex-auto-install":true,"latex-clean":true,"latex-min-runs":1,"latex-max-runs":10,"latex-makeindex":"makeindex","latex-makeindex-opts":[],"latex-tlmgr-opts":[],"latex-input-paths":[],"latex-output-dir":null,"link-external-icon":false,"link-external-newwindow":false,"self-contained-math":false,"format-resources":[],"notebook-links":true},"pandoc":{"standalone":true,"wrap":"none","default-image-extension":"png","to